258
00:12:18,920 --> 00:12:21,780
because we actually have ice that we've frozen in here

259
00:12:21,781 --> 00:12:24,220
here during the night. I'm actually tapping the ice

260
00:12:24,221 --> 00:12:27,580
right here. During the day, when the air

261
00:12:27,581 --> 00:12:31,180
conditioning unit is turned on, the pump sends warm refrigerant

262
00:12:31,181 --> 00:12:35,320
back through the coils and through the ice.

263
00:12:35,321 --> 00:12:38,300
That refrigerant is chilled and then pumped through pipes into

264
00:12:38,301 --> 00:12:43,640
the existing air conditioning system to provide cooling.

265
00:12:43,641 --> 00:12:46,740
At the end of the day, the ice bear goes into hibernation,

266
00:12:46,741 --> 00:12:50,880
until later that night when the process begins again.

267
00:12:50,881 --> 00:12:53,120
we'll be able to provide about eight hours worth of cooling

268
00:12:53,121 --> 00:12:54,120
out of this ice.

269
00:12:54,121 --> 00:12:56,280
And then, again, it will all be water,

270
00:12:56,281 --> 00:13:01,060
and we'll use the condensing unit again to freeze it into ice.

271
00:13:01,061 --> 00:13:03,220
By getting its energy at night instead of mauling

272
00:13:03,221 --> 00:13:06,460
the power grid during the day, the ice bear can put the deep

273
00:13:06,461 --> 00:13:10,160
freeze on greenhouse gases by using cheaper, cleaner

274
00:13:10,161 --> 00:13:12,300
nighttime energy.

275
00:13:12,301 --> 00:13:15,240
A lot of places are now generating renewable energy

276
00:13:15,241 --> 00:13:16,880
at night with wind.

277
00:13:16,881 --> 00:13:19,480
So by moving some of that demand to the nighttime,

278
00:13:19,481 --> 00:13:20,940
You're also moving some of that demand

279
00:13:20,941 --> 00:13:23,540
to cleaner sources of energy that help the environment.

280
00:13:23,860 --> 00:13:25,620
This technology is so simple.

281
00:13:26,060 --> 00:13:30,280
It's a huge block of ice that is outside our library,

282
00:13:30,460 --> 00:13:33,940
and somehow it's working to keep this building cool.

283
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:37,740
One city that could benefit from rooftop wind turbines

284
00:13:37,741 --> 00:13:39,360
is Milton, Massachusetts.

285
00:13:39,980 --> 00:13:43,480
With average annual winds of 15.4 miles per hour,

286
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:47,140
Milton is the breeziest city in the United States.

287
00:13:49,480 --> 00:13:56,480
In the desert outside Seville, Spain,

288
00:13:56,580 --> 00:14:01,020
360 feet of green technology is catching some rays.

289
00:14:01,120 --> 00:14:05,560
It may be the method that brings a massive infusion

290
00:14:05,660 --> 00:14:08,040
of solar energy to our cities.

291
00:14:08,140 --> 00:14:11,140
It's called a solar tower, and it

292
00:14:11,240 --> 00:14:13,900
stands as a beacon of hope in the fight against global warming.

293
00:14:16,080 --> 00:14:18,200
The top of the concrete tower basks

294
00:14:18,201 --> 00:14:23,200
basks in the reflection of a sea of mirrors called heliostats.

295
00:14:23,201 --> 00:14:28,200
The heliostats are spread over 170 acres, and every day the sun

296
00:14:28,201 --> 00:14:33,200
shines, it turns the tower into an oracle of solar energy.

297
00:14:33,201 --> 00:14:37,200
Valerio Fernandez is the plant's engineering director.

298
00:14:37,201 --> 00:14:40,200
We are at the middle of the heliostat field of the

299
00:14:40,201 --> 00:14:44,200
Piastren Solar Plant. It is composed by 624 heliostats

300
00:14:44,201 --> 00:14:48,200
that are composed by a mirror. The function of this

301
00:14:48,201 --> 00:14:50,200
is to reflect solar radiation.

302
00:14:50,300 --> 00:14:52,940
These units are not hot.

303
00:14:53,040 --> 00:14:55,200
The heliostats are programmed to keep the heat

304
00:14:55,300 --> 00:14:58,340
on the tower from dawn to dusk.

305
00:14:58,440 --> 00:15:01,840
As the Earth turns, the heliostats move in accordance,

306
00:15:01,940 --> 00:15:05,860
staying perfectly focused between the sun and the tower.

307
00:15:05,960 --> 00:15:08,960
There's years that have 29 days in February,

308
00:15:09,060 --> 00:15:11,260
so all the years are different.

309
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:15,460
And the equations to reflect very accurately the movement

310
00:15:15,560 --> 00:15:17,960
of the sun are complex.

311
00:15:17,961 --> 00:15:22,600
and have to be updated every several years.

312
00:15:22,601 --> 00:15:26,560
Each heliostat is made of glass and steel,

313
00:15:26,561 --> 00:15:30,800
with a surface area of 1,300 square feet.

314
00:15:30,801 --> 00:15:33,440
They are made of mirrors, conventional mirrors,

315
00:15:33,441 --> 00:15:36,720
that reflect solar radiation, and they are slightly curved

316
00:15:36,721 --> 00:15:40,120
in order to concentrate solar radiation towards a point

317
00:15:40,121 --> 00:15:44,280
that is the receiver that is placed in the top of the tower.

318
00:15:44,281 --> 00:15:46,320
Under the force of all the mirrors,

319
00:15:46,321 --> 00:15:50,160
the solar receiver reaches temperatures of 1,000 degrees

320
00:15:50,260 --> 00:15:51,260
Fahrenheit.

321
00:15:51,360 --> 00:15:53,060
That's hotter than the daytime temperature

322
00:15:53,160 --> 00:15:55,960
on the planet Mercury.

323
00:15:56,060 --> 00:15:58,700
The receiver is made of four steel panels.

324
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:02,300
Each panel is an array of pipes welded together.

325
00:16:02,400 --> 00:16:04,780
Within the pipes is water.

326
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:07,480
When the sun hits the panels, the water is heated

327
00:16:07,580 --> 00:16:09,840
and turned into steam.

328
00:16:09,940 --> 00:16:12,080
Pressure created from the steam's expansion

329
00:16:12,180 --> 00:16:16,320
drives the turbine, which creates electricity.

330
00:16:16,321 --> 00:16:18,620
Once that electricity moves into the power grid,

331
00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:22,120
the process is complete. The sun's heat

332
00:16:22,220 --> 00:16:24,360
has been successfully harnessed and turned

333
00:16:24,460 --> 00:16:28,560
into clean energy. This single tower

334
00:16:28,660 --> 00:16:31,760
provides energy for 6,000 homes.

335
00:16:31,860 --> 00:16:33,680
But the Spanish aren't stopping there.

336
00:16:33,780 --> 00:16:36,480
They're building more towers. In fact, they've planned

337
00:16:36,580 --> 00:16:39,880
a total of nine. The entire project

338
00:16:39,980 --> 00:16:43,920
will produce enough power for the 700,000 residents 12 miles

339
00:16:43,921 --> 00:16:48,920
miles away in Seville. This spark for tower technology

340
00:16:48,921 --> 00:16:53,860
was ignited in the California desert during the 1980s.

341
00:16:53,861 --> 00:16:57,360
Built as a full-scale prototype, Solar One

342
00:16:57,361 --> 00:17:01,080
harnessed the power of 1,800 mirrors, all aimed at the top

343
00:17:01,081 --> 00:17:05,980
of a 295-foot tower. But instead of using water

344
00:17:05,981 --> 00:17:10,380
as a heat conduit, Solar One used oil.

345
00:17:10,381 --> 00:17:13,920
For four years, the prototype sporadically produced up to 10

346
00:17:13,921 --> 00:17:19,820
megawatts of electricity, enough to power about 5,000 homes.

347
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:23,760
But more importantly, it proved the technology worked.

348
00:17:23,860 --> 00:17:26,800
I don't think anyone believed that there would be so many

349
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:29,840
days and so many kilowatts of energy generated.

350
00:17:29,940 --> 00:17:33,680
It actually exceeded all of the estimates that were initially

351
00:17:33,780 --> 00:17:35,440
done.

352
00:17:35,540 --> 00:17:38,840
Back in Seville, Spain, engineers chose

353
00:17:38,940 --> 00:17:42,240
water as a heat exchanger.

354
00:17:42,241 --> 00:17:45,380
But there's still one major challenge to overcome,

355
00:17:45,381 --> 00:17:47,480
how to keep the turbine consistently spinning

356
00:17:47,481 --> 00:17:49,480
when it's dark.

357
00:17:49,481 --> 00:17:51,440
At this moment, our first plants

358
00:17:51,441 --> 00:17:55,060
have only a very limited storage capacity

359
00:17:55,061 --> 00:17:58,620
of thermal energy that allows us to produce electricity

360
00:17:58,621 --> 00:17:59,920
when there is no sun.

361
00:17:59,921 --> 00:18:04,060
But this is limited to about one hour.

362
00:18:04,061 --> 00:18:06,060
The trick is in how to keep water hot enough

363
00:18:06,061 --> 00:18:08,600
during the night to produce the steam that drives the turbine.

364
00:18:12,240 --> 00:18:15,840
So engineers are looking at hybrid solutions, like wind

365
00:18:15,940 --> 00:18:19,040
power and biofuels, as ways to keep the clean energy flowing

366
00:18:19,140 --> 00:18:21,040
24-7.

367
00:18:21,140 --> 00:18:24,480
Still, the Spanish solar tower is glowing with success.

368
00:18:26,580 --> 00:18:30,500
When the sun shines, the tower produces a daily dose of 11

369
00:18:30,600 --> 00:18:32,900
megawatts of electricity.

370
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:36,040
And it's done without a single puff of greenhouse gas

371
00:18:36,140 --> 00:18:37,440
emission.

372
00:18:37,540 --> 00:18:40,540
With this successful commercial plant,

373
00:18:40,541 --> 00:18:45,040
commercial plant, I think we did the very successful first step.

374
00:18:45,041 --> 00:18:48,640
The proven long-term success of the Spanish project has created

375
00:18:48,641 --> 00:18:52,080
a solar tower awakening.

376
00:18:52,081 --> 00:18:54,720
From China to Morocco, tower power

377
00:18:54,721 --> 00:18:56,960
is beginning to capture the attention of investors

378
00:18:56,961 --> 00:18:59,500
and government officials.

379
00:18:59,501 --> 00:19:02,560
But not all solar towers are created equal.

380
00:19:02,561 --> 00:19:05,900
There's yet another very interesting solar thermal

381
00:19:05,901 --> 00:19:06,900
technology.

382
00:19:06,901 --> 00:19:10,300
That's where you simply take an upside-down funnel

383
00:19:10,301 --> 00:19:15,600
and allow the sun to pound on that funnel all day long.

384
00:19:15,700 --> 00:19:17,340
Not surprisingly, the funneled model

385
00:19:17,440 --> 00:19:22,040
was first tested in eco-friendly Spain, just outside Madrid,

386
00:19:22,140 --> 00:19:25,540
in sun-drenched Manzanares.

387
00:19:25,640 --> 00:19:29,020
The project was based on a simple principle of physics.

388
00:19:29,120 --> 00:19:32,780
Hot air rises.

389
00:19:32,880 --> 00:19:35,620
The plant started with a massive greenhouse.

390
00:19:35,720 --> 00:19:38,520
A clear polymer canopy, or collection area,

391
00:19:38,521 --> 00:19:43,520
surrounded a 630-foot tower with a hollow bottom.

392
00:19:43,620 --> 00:19:45,620
As the sun beat down on the canopy,

393
00:19:45,720 --> 00:19:48,520
the air underneath was heated to 95 degrees

394
00:19:48,620 --> 00:19:51,500
above the ambient temperature.

395
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:53,540
The heated air moved toward the cooler air

396
00:19:53,640 --> 00:19:57,080
at the top of the tower.

397
00:19:57,180 --> 00:20:00,100
That updraft funneled through a large turbine stationed

398
00:20:00,200 --> 00:20:00,900
inside the tower.

399
00:20:03,780 --> 00:20:07,480
The plant operated from 1982 through 1989

400
00:20:07,481 --> 00:20:09,540
and put out 50 kilowatts.

401
00:20:09,640 --> 00:20:12,520
Not much, but it worked.

402
00:20:12,620 --> 00:20:15,520
The design seemed ready for its day in the sun.

403
00:20:15,620 --> 00:20:20,220
But in the late 1980s, oil was cheap and interest chilled.

404
00:20:20,320 --> 00:20:22,060
We've learned that there's a couple of things

405
00:20:22,160 --> 00:20:24,860
that drive investments in solar towers.

406
00:20:24,960 --> 00:20:27,100
One is the cost of oil.

407
00:20:27,200 --> 00:20:30,140
The other thing, of course, is government policy.

408
00:20:30,240 --> 00:20:32,400
Now with oil prices at record highs

409
00:20:32,500 --> 00:20:35,780
and environmental issues burning in the public's consciousness,

410
00:20:35,781 --> 00:20:40,180
Interest in the Manzanares model has reached critical mass.

411
00:20:40,280 --> 00:20:43,220
An Australian firm called EnviroMission

412
00:20:43,320 --> 00:20:46,320
plans to take a new tower to unprecedented heights.

413
00:20:46,420 --> 00:20:48,520
It will be one of the largest structures, if not the largest

414
00:20:48,620 --> 00:20:50,520
structure on the face of the Earth.

415
00:20:50,620 --> 00:20:52,540
The steel-reinforced concrete tower

416
00:20:52,640 --> 00:20:56,040
will rise 2,500 feet above the Earth.

417
00:20:56,140 --> 00:20:58,980
That's nearly half a mile and twice the height of the Empire

418
00:20:59,080 --> 00:20:59,780
State Building.

419
00:20:59,781 --> 00:21:09,300
Its base will be 430 feet in diameter, wide enough to provide a firm anchor during high winds.

420
00:21:11,260 --> 00:21:14,300
The glass and polymer canopy will span three miles,

421
00:21:14,980 --> 00:21:19,220
gently sloping from a height of 6 feet at the perimeter to 100 feet at the tower.

422
00:21:20,560 --> 00:21:26,500
There, 32 jet-sized turbines will be waiting to take the hot air and turn it into electricity.

423
00:21:26,501 --> 00:21:30,500
The tower promises year-round energy.

424
00:21:30,501 --> 00:21:32,500
Greenhouses still operate in winter,

425
00:21:32,501 --> 00:21:34,500
so they still collect warm air.

426
00:21:34,501 --> 00:21:36,500
You still create a temperature differential.

427
00:21:36,501 --> 00:21:40,500
You may not have the huge output you have in summer,

428
00:21:40,501 --> 00:21:43,500
but you will certainly have a very substantial output.

429
00:21:43,501 --> 00:21:46,500
EnviroMission believes their tower will light up the power

430
00:21:46,501 --> 00:21:50,500
grid with an astounding 200 megawatts.

431
00:21:50,501 --> 00:21:53,500
Even if the tower runs at 80% capacity,

432
00:21:53,501 --> 00:21:58,740
capacity, that's enough power to fuel half a million households.

433
00:21:58,840 --> 00:22:01,980
EnviroMission has already purchased a 20,000 acre site

434
00:22:02,080 --> 00:22:04,040
between Melbourne and Sydney.

435
00:22:04,140 --> 00:22:08,180
It'll be an engineering marvel and obviously

436
00:22:08,280 --> 00:22:10,080
a renewable energy icon.

437
00:22:10,180 --> 00:22:12,180
And it will be expensive.

438
00:22:12,280 --> 00:22:17,220
Estimated cost to build the tower is $700 million.

439
00:22:17,320 --> 00:22:19,800
It's a hefty price, but one that would eventually

440
00:22:19,900 --> 00:22:21,720
pay for itself.

441
00:22:21,721 --> 00:22:25,760
It will be, we believe, competitive in output with coal,

442
00:22:26,060 --> 00:22:28,260
but it is more expensive to build,

443
00:22:29,420 --> 00:22:31,680
but it costs nothing to run.

444
00:22:32,100 --> 00:22:34,920
With investment capital rushing toward green machines,

445
00:22:35,360 --> 00:22:37,280
that $700 million looks attainable,

446
00:22:38,280 --> 00:22:40,340
Jetson-style transport module included.

447
00:22:40,880 --> 00:22:42,820
I think this is the holy grail of renewable energy.

448
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:45,680
Opportunistic, yes, obviously.

449
00:22:45,800 --> 00:22:47,080
I mean, you don't do anything for nothing,

450
00:22:47,340 --> 00:22:49,560
but what was a dream became a passion.

451
00:22:50,220 --> 00:22:51,640
We are going to get it built.

452
00:22:51,720 --> 00:22:55,120
Producing enough power for all the households in the United

453
00:22:55,220 --> 00:22:59,260
States would require over 620 solar towers the size of

454
00:22:59,360 --> 00:23:01,260
EnviroMission's design.

455
00:23:01,360 --> 00:23:05,660
Of those, California would need 65 and Wyoming just two.

456
00:23:15,720 --> 00:23:19,080
Listen to the distinct sound of this pollution solution.

457
00:23:21,720 --> 00:23:29,680
It's a car that runs on compressed air, called a CAT, for compressed air technology.

458
00:23:29,681 --> 00:23:35,160
It's the result of 14 years of research and development by French engine designer Guy

459
00:23:35,161 --> 00:23:43,080
Negra. With the CAT, Negra has created a car that produces zero emissions.

460
00:23:47,080 --> 00:23:51,700
The premise of this project was that in order to run a car, particularly in town,

461
00:23:51,701 --> 00:23:53,500
you need very little energy.

462
00:23:53,600 --> 00:23:56,300
And with compressed air, you can accumulate enough energy

463
00:23:56,400 --> 00:23:57,100
to do that.

464
00:24:00,400 --> 00:24:02,940
Compressed air has long been used as a power source

465
00:24:03,040 --> 00:24:04,540
for various tools and equipment.

466
00:24:07,240 --> 00:24:08,860
For car designers, compressed air

467
00:24:08,960 --> 00:24:11,460
has been difficult to harness.

468
00:24:11,560 --> 00:24:15,700
But now, Negra and his team at Motor Development International

469
00:24:15,800 --> 00:24:18,360
have built an engine that they believe will blow the car

470
00:24:18,460 --> 00:24:19,300
market away.

471
00:24:21,700 --> 00:24:25,740
Within an internal combustion engine, gasoline and oxygen

472
00:24:25,840 --> 00:24:26,940
ignite.

473
00:24:27,040 --> 00:24:29,440
The resulting explosions create pressure, which

474
00:24:29,540 --> 00:24:31,840
pushes the pistons that turn the crankshaft.

475
00:24:31,940 --> 00:24:35,940
With MDI's engine, precisely timed bursts of compressed air

476
00:24:36,040 --> 00:24:39,180
create the pressure needed to drive the pistons, pollutant

477
00:24:39,280 --> 00:24:39,980
free.

478
00:24:42,720 --> 00:24:45,560
Supplying enough energy to power the car requires pressure

479
00:24:45,660 --> 00:24:49,560
at 4,300 pounds per square inch.

480
00:24:49,561 --> 00:24:51,800
That's more than 100 times the pressure

481
00:24:51,900 --> 00:24:54,160
in a standard car tire.

482
00:24:54,260 --> 00:24:57,000
Forced inside a tank small enough to fit under the cat's

483
00:24:57,100 --> 00:25:01,680
chassis, it's the formula for a potential ticking time bomb.

484
00:25:01,780 --> 00:25:04,140
So the pressure was on the designers

485
00:25:04,240 --> 00:25:06,780
to build a tank that won't explode.

486
00:25:06,880 --> 00:25:09,440
This is to give you an idea of the technology

487
00:25:09,540 --> 00:25:10,740
inside of the car.

488
00:25:10,840 --> 00:25:13,420
You can see that under the floor, you have some carbon fiber

489
00:25:13,520 --> 00:25:14,480
tanks.

490
00:25:14,580 --> 00:25:17,960
It has to be carbon fiber on cars because it is very safe.

491
00:25:17,961 --> 00:25:19,460
If there is a crash or something,

492
00:25:19,560 --> 00:25:21,360
there's no problem with pieces going everywhere.

493
00:25:21,460 --> 00:25:25,300
There is a crack, and then the air goes out.

494
00:25:25,400 --> 00:25:26,760
The tanks can even withstand

495
00:25:26,860 --> 00:25:30,200
the force of small caliber gunfire.

496
00:25:30,300 --> 00:25:32,840
Since it's being powered by air, the cat

497
00:25:32,940 --> 00:25:37,920
has a lightweight aluminum frame to keep it lean and nimble.

498
00:25:38,020 --> 00:25:40,920
The same care went into the body.

499
00:25:41,020 --> 00:25:44,720
The body of the car will be made with fiberglass.

500
00:25:44,820 --> 00:25:46,960
Using fiberglass is very important for us

501
00:25:46,961 --> 00:25:48,460
because the car will be lighter.

502
00:25:48,560 --> 00:25:51,140
And having a light car, you will have more range.

503
00:25:51,240 --> 00:25:54,300
In our technology, we won't store a lot of energy.

504
00:25:54,400 --> 00:25:58,140
So we have to manage this energy to have a bigger range.

505
00:25:58,240 --> 00:26:01,700
MDI has licensed the technology to Tata Motors,

506
00:26:01,800 --> 00:26:04,440
India's largest car manufacturer.

507
00:26:04,540 --> 00:26:07,080
And Mexico City is looking at the cat as a way

508
00:26:07,180 --> 00:26:10,080
to cut into its layer of smog.

509
00:26:10,180 --> 00:26:14,080
Once the cat leaves the factory, it will need some air.

510
00:26:14,081 --> 00:26:17,920
An MDI will need to convince gas stations to play along

511
00:26:18,020 --> 00:26:22,120
by installing industrial compressors and air tanks.

512
00:26:22,220 --> 00:26:26,120
This is a compressor, and you can use this to fill some

513
00:26:26,220 --> 00:26:28,920
high-pressure tanks, a huge volume.

514
00:26:29,020 --> 00:26:30,840
And then when a car arrives at the air station,

515
00:26:30,940 --> 00:26:33,140
you just have to transfer the air from these big tanks

516
00:26:33,240 --> 00:26:37,040
to the car, and that takes just three minutes.

517
00:26:37,140 --> 00:26:40,180
But airing up can also be done at home.

518
00:26:40,280 --> 00:26:42,640
Plug in a compressor that comes with a car,

519
00:26:42,641 --> 00:26:48,680
the car, and in three to four hours, the tank is full.

520
00:26:48,681 --> 00:26:54,280
It's now ready to go 125 miles for just two bucks.

521
00:26:54,281 --> 00:26:57,060
If the air is generated with a renewable source,

522
00:26:57,061 --> 00:26:58,820
the car becomes a true green machine.

523
00:27:02,800 --> 00:27:05,640
This is the motor alternator to fill the car

524
00:27:05,641 --> 00:27:09,060
when you plug the car on an electric plug to fill the tanks.

525
00:27:09,061 --> 00:27:12,640
Put it all together, and the city version, or mini-cat,

526
00:27:12,641 --> 00:27:14,640
hit a top speed of 70 miles per hour.

527
00:27:17,940 --> 00:27:19,840
If you want a Tiger in your tank,

528
00:27:19,841 --> 00:27:23,720
MDI has developed a gasoline dual energy version.

529
00:27:23,721 --> 00:27:26,380
When the air is depleted, gasoline drives the engine

530
00:27:26,381 --> 00:27:30,620
while an onboard compressor refills the air tank.

531
00:27:30,621 --> 00:27:32,800
Alternating between the two fuels,

532
00:27:32,801 --> 00:27:36,300
the hybrid will hiss along for an amazing 600 miles

533
00:27:36,301 --> 00:27:39,760
on one small tank of gasoline.

534
00:27:39,761 --> 00:27:41,840
The first car targeted for early production

535
00:27:41,841 --> 00:27:43,540
as the minicat.

536
00:27:43,541 --> 00:27:45,980
If you think it looks strange from the outside,

537
00:27:45,981 --> 00:27:48,180
take a closer look at the steering wheel.

538
00:27:48,181 --> 00:27:50,720
This is why we've put the driver in the middle.

539
00:27:50,721 --> 00:27:52,060
Having the driver in the middle, you

540
00:27:52,061 --> 00:27:54,220
can put it a little bit more in the front of the car,

541
00:27:54,221 --> 00:27:55,880
so you have more volume for the trunk.

542
00:27:55,881 --> 00:27:57,580
This is the basic idea.

543
00:27:57,581 --> 00:27:59,920
But then also, for us, it's more simple to use this car

544
00:27:59,921 --> 00:28:01,900
in England and France, for example.

545
00:28:01,901 --> 00:28:06,100
There's no problem, because the driver is in the middle.

546
00:28:06,101 --> 00:28:08,540
Right side steering, left side steering,

547
00:28:08,541 --> 00:28:10,200
it doesn't matter.

548
00:28:10,201 --> 00:28:14,100
Guy Negra has made a car for everyone.

549
00:28:14,200 --> 00:28:17,640
Costs for the cats will range from $7,000 for a four-cylinder

550
00:28:17,740 --> 00:28:21,740
minicat to $25,000 for a six-cylinder family car.

551
00:28:25,240 --> 00:28:28,360
Negra's determination to be eco-responsible extends to

552
00:28:28,460 --> 00:28:30,860
manufacturing as well.

553
00:28:30,960 --> 00:28:35,560
We are going to expand the industrial concept we came up

554
00:28:35,660 --> 00:28:39,260
with, which is to make cars in small factories located within

555
00:28:39,261 --> 00:28:40,500
in the market.

556
00:28:40,501 --> 00:28:42,260
It is an original concept.

557
00:28:42,261 --> 00:28:45,840
It's very important for the economy, for social issues,

558
00:28:45,841 --> 00:28:47,700
pollution issues, and the environment.

559
00:28:51,560 --> 00:28:54,300
The compressed air car works.

560
00:28:54,301 --> 00:28:57,660
Now all Guy Negrin needs is the support of the public

561
00:28:57,661 --> 00:29:00,960
and the automobile industry.

562
00:29:00,961 --> 00:29:03,580
Breakthroughs are always difficult, especially

563
00:29:03,581 --> 00:29:07,400
technological breakthroughs, because the industry generally

564
00:29:07,401 --> 00:29:10,840
follows the direction that has already been established.

565
00:29:10,841 --> 00:29:12,480
And they don't like to see someone

566
00:29:12,481 --> 00:29:14,420
trying to change the course.

567
00:29:14,421 --> 00:29:17,640
But today, there is a worldwide need to change the course.

568
00:29:20,980 --> 00:29:23,640
The average American car creates 10,000 pounds

569
00:29:23,641 --> 00:29:26,000
of carbon dioxide each year.

570
00:29:26,001 --> 00:29:29,280
That's nearly one quarter of the country's entire CO2 output.

571
00:29:29,281 --> 00:29:40,520
We now return to Environmental Tech 2 on Modern Marvels.

572
00:29:42,520 --> 00:29:44,580
Water and the American West.

573
00:29:45,620 --> 00:29:47,180
There's only so much of it.

574
00:29:48,260 --> 00:29:50,640
But underneath one of the thirstiest regions

575
00:29:50,641 --> 00:29:54,140
in Southern California, there's a trillion gallons of it.

576
00:29:54,700 --> 00:29:57,760
And a lot of it is polluted, but not for long.

577
00:29:57,761 --> 00:30:00,960
200 feet below the San Gabriel Valley,

578
00:30:01,060 --> 00:30:04,400
toxic chemicals have contaminated the aquifer.

579
00:30:04,500 --> 00:30:06,160
They've been here for decades.

580
00:30:06,260 --> 00:30:08,300
Back in the beginning of the 1900s,

581
00:30:08,400 --> 00:30:10,100
the beginning of the Industrial Revolution,

582
00:30:10,200 --> 00:30:12,200
we thought that we could throw things away.

583
00:30:12,300 --> 00:30:14,920
We thought we could throw waste into rivers

584
00:30:15,020 --> 00:30:17,420
and waste materials into landfills

585
00:30:17,520 --> 00:30:20,420
or dump things into the ocean, and that it would go away.

586
00:30:20,520 --> 00:30:24,320
And before the 1970s, none of the laws

587
00:30:24,321 --> 00:30:29,160
that are in effect today were around, and practices for

588
00:30:29,161 --> 00:30:31,960
handling and disposing of chemicals were much different

589
00:30:31,961 --> 00:30:34,960
than they are today. It took one of the worst

590
00:30:34,961 --> 00:30:39,360
ecological disasters in American history to force that change,

591
00:30:39,361 --> 00:30:44,880
Love Canal. In 1942, an upstate New York

592
00:30:44,881 --> 00:30:48,880
waterway became a toxic waste dump used by a chemical and

593
00:30:48,881 --> 00:30:53,280
plastics company. By 1978, nearby residents were

594
00:30:53,281 --> 00:30:56,120
were suffering with abnormal rates of birth defects,

595
00:30:56,220 --> 00:31:00,520
miscarriages, and cancer. From this tragedy came the

596
00:31:00,620 --> 00:31:04,660
Superfund, a federal program intended to identify and clean

597
00:31:04,760 --> 00:31:08,100
up toxic waste sites across America.

598
00:31:08,200 --> 00:31:11,360
For the first time, technology would be used on a massive scale

599
00:31:11,460 --> 00:31:14,460
to systematically clean up the environment.

600
00:31:14,560 --> 00:31:17,740
The San Gabriel Aquifer is one of those sites.

601
00:31:17,840 --> 00:31:20,080
The EPA has identified about 20 companies

602
00:31:20,180 --> 00:31:23,260
that we think have contributed to the pollution in the

603
00:31:23,280 --> 00:31:26,880
area. The responsible parties

604
00:31:26,881 --> 00:31:29,580
range from industrial cleaning companies to rocket

605
00:31:29,581 --> 00:31:34,820
manufacturers. Every day, 11 million gallons,

606
00:31:34,821 --> 00:31:39,060
about 500 swimming pools worth of contaminated aquifer water

607
00:31:39,061 --> 00:31:43,000
enters the San Gabriel water treatment plant for a cleaning.

608
00:31:43,001 --> 00:31:46,200
The water is pulled out of the aquifer through three

609
00:31:46,201 --> 00:31:49,040
extraction wells, which feed this treatment plant.

610
00:31:49,041 --> 00:31:51,540
The water goes into the first phase of treatment, which is

611
00:31:51,541 --> 00:31:54,680
air stripping through that vertical pipe going up the side

612
00:31:54,780 --> 00:31:56,680
of the air stripping tower.

613
00:31:56,780 --> 00:31:59,380
The water goes into the top of the tower,

614
00:31:59,480 --> 00:32:01,780
and it falls through.

615
00:32:01,880 --> 00:32:03,520
The tanks are filled with thousands

616
00:32:03,620 --> 00:32:06,680
of grapefruit-sized plastic balls.

617
00:32:06,780 --> 00:32:09,160
Each ball is a porous matrix.

618
00:32:09,260 --> 00:32:11,020
As the water falls through the balls,

619
00:32:11,120 --> 00:32:13,800
it's broken up into droplets.

620
00:32:13,900 --> 00:32:17,300
Meanwhile, air is introduced from the bottom of the tank.

621
00:32:17,400 --> 00:32:20,260
When the air meets the droplets, volatile contaminants

622
00:32:20,261 --> 00:32:24,100
vapor and are carried out the top of the tower.

623
00:32:24,200 --> 00:32:29,000
The air with the volatilized compounds leaves the top of the

624
00:32:29,100 --> 00:32:33,880
tower and goes into the blue bins, which are carbon filter

625
00:32:33,980 --> 00:32:35,000
bins.

626
00:32:35,100 --> 00:32:37,940
The air enters the top of the bin, moves through the carbon

627
00:32:38,040 --> 00:32:42,420
bed, and then exits out the bottom and up the stack as clean

628
00:32:42,520 --> 00:32:44,420
air.

629
00:32:44,520 --> 00:32:46,380
While air stripping removes most of the

630
00:32:46,381 --> 00:32:48,140
of the volatile chemicals.

631
00:32:48,240 --> 00:32:51,480
Several other contaminants remain.

632
00:32:51,580 --> 00:32:54,820
Next on the list for removal is a potential cancer-causing

633
00:32:54,920 --> 00:32:58,920
agent, 1,2,3-trichloropropane.

634
00:32:59,020 --> 00:33:00,820
After the water leaves the air strippers,

635
00:33:00,920 --> 00:33:03,300
it enters through this top pipe here

636
00:33:03,400 --> 00:33:06,640
into these 10 vessels, which are two-thirds filled

637
00:33:06,740 --> 00:33:09,640
with granular carbon, which is this material here.

638
00:33:09,740 --> 00:33:12,080
The water enters through the top of the tanks,

639
00:33:12,180 --> 00:33:13,240
goes through the carbon.

640
00:33:13,340 --> 00:33:15,180
Any volatiles that either were not

641
00:33:15,181 --> 00:33:18,180
removed by the air strippers, and as well as the 1, 2, 3

642
00:33:18,280 --> 00:33:20,880
trichloropropane are removed.

643
00:33:20,980 --> 00:33:24,480
The clean water then exits the system

644
00:33:24,580 --> 00:33:27,360
through this bottom line here and goes on to the next phase

645
00:33:27,460 --> 00:33:30,800
of treatment, which is ion exchange.

646
00:33:30,900 --> 00:33:33,660
At the ion exchange station, the water flows

647
00:33:33,760 --> 00:33:35,860
through a series of tanks.

648
00:33:35,960 --> 00:33:38,800
The target here is a component of solid rocket fuel

649
00:33:38,900 --> 00:33:40,940
called perchlorate.

650
00:33:41,040 --> 00:33:45,180
If enough is ingested, perchlorate attacks the thyroid.

651
00:33:45,181 --> 00:33:49,080
They're filled with the ion exchange resin,

652
00:33:49,180 --> 00:33:51,320
this synthetic resin.

653
00:33:51,420 --> 00:33:55,160
There are 30 vessels on each carousel.

654
00:33:55,260 --> 00:33:57,660
As the water containing perchlorate

655
00:33:57,760 --> 00:34:00,520
passes through the vessels filled with resin,

656
00:34:00,620 --> 00:34:03,040
the resin grabs onto the perchlorate ions

657
00:34:03,140 --> 00:34:05,940
and replaces them with chloride ion.

658
00:34:06,040 --> 00:34:09,040
One last stubborn chemical remains,

659
00:34:09,140 --> 00:34:13,600
the potentially cancer-causing NDMA.

660
00:34:13,601 --> 00:34:15,840
The best way to eliminate it is by zapping it

661
00:34:15,940 --> 00:34:16,940
with ultraviolet light.

662
00:34:20,240 --> 00:34:23,340
This is the last step in our treatment facility.

663
00:34:23,440 --> 00:34:27,240
This is the ultraviolet, or UV, system.

664
00:34:27,340 --> 00:34:29,440
The water flows through here.

665
00:34:29,540 --> 00:34:33,560
Each one of these panels has 16 lights.

666
00:34:33,660 --> 00:34:35,760
The water flows through this light,

667
00:34:35,860 --> 00:34:39,500
and the NDMA is inactivated.

668
00:34:39,600 --> 00:34:42,860
NDMA is a component of rocket fuel.

669
00:34:42,861 --> 00:34:46,660
And this is what it looks like.

670
00:34:46,760 --> 00:34:49,060
Inside the trough, the water passes

671
00:34:49,160 --> 00:34:53,160
through a series of 2,200 ultraviolet lights.

672
00:34:53,260 --> 00:34:55,900
As the NDMA molecules absorb the light,

673
00:34:56,000 --> 00:35:00,920
they're broken down into non-toxic components.

674
00:35:01,020 --> 00:35:02,420
At the end of the tank, the water

675
00:35:02,520 --> 00:35:04,620
gets a little dash of chlorine and then

676
00:35:04,720 --> 00:35:08,960
makes a clean break for the city water supply.

677
00:35:09,060 --> 00:35:11,120
Here we are at the effluent of the plant.

678
00:35:11,121 --> 00:35:14,520
What less than one hour ago entered the plant as contaminated

679
00:35:14,620 --> 00:35:19,960
water is now drinking water, safe for public consumption.

680
00:35:23,000 --> 00:35:25,900
As the clean water leaves this part of the plant,

681
00:35:26,000 --> 00:35:27,640
an equal amount of contaminated water

682
00:35:27,740 --> 00:35:30,880
will enter at the intake.

683
00:35:30,980 --> 00:35:35,480
It's a cycle that will repeat for decades to come.

684
00:35:35,580 --> 00:35:37,280
The area of groundwater contamination

685
00:35:37,380 --> 00:35:40,380
is more than eight miles long and a mile wide.

686
00:35:40,381 --> 00:35:45,820
We think it took 40 to 50 years to become that large.

687
00:35:45,920 --> 00:35:47,720
It's going to take as long or longer

688
00:35:47,820 --> 00:35:50,520
to clean the groundwater up.

689
00:35:50,620 --> 00:35:53,920
To date, at this plant alone, the cleanup has cost a total

690
00:35:54,020 --> 00:35:57,140
of $48 million and counting.

691
00:35:57,240 --> 00:36:01,040
Of that, the federal government has paid about 30%.

692
00:36:01,140 --> 00:36:04,640
The responsible parties have paid the rest.

693
00:36:04,740 --> 00:36:09,080
The EPA has identified nearly 1,600 contaminated sites.

694
00:36:09,081 --> 00:36:13,080
Of those, nearly 1,400 have polluted groundwater.

695
00:36:13,180 --> 00:36:16,080
Currently, one in four Americans lives within three

696
00:36:16,180 --> 00:36:18,080
miles of a Superfund site.

697
00:36:26,040 --> 00:36:28,640
We now return to Environmental Tech II

698
00:36:28,740 --> 00:36:32,040
on Modern Marvels.

699
00:36:32,140 --> 00:36:35,480
Aboard the Weatherbird II in Fort Lauderdale, Florida,

700
00:36:35,580 --> 00:36:38,840
a crew of marine biologists is preparing for a journey

701
00:36:38,841 --> 00:36:43,080
that they believe will help save a sick ocean, and at the same time,

702
00:36:43,081 --> 00:36:46,840
help solve global warming by turning the ocean into a giant

703
00:36:46,841 --> 00:36:50,360
sponge. The biologists will target an

704
00:36:50,361 --> 00:36:54,360
indispensable element for human life, plankton.

705
00:36:54,361 --> 00:36:57,160
Some of these microscopic organisms can act as floating

706
00:36:57,161 --> 00:37:01,320
forests, gobbling up carbon dioxide from the air and returning

707
00:37:01,321 --> 00:37:06,040
it as pure oxygen. But the plankton beds are shrinking

708
00:37:06,041 --> 00:37:10,780
at a rate of 1% a year. The weatherbird crew

709
00:37:10,880 --> 00:37:13,480
is determined to reverse that trend with a simple

710
00:37:13,580 --> 00:37:16,380
but controversial experiment.

711
00:37:16,480 --> 00:37:18,680
Planktose, our company is in the business

712
00:37:18,780 --> 00:37:21,580
of eco-restoration. We say that we restore the trees

713
00:37:21,680 --> 00:37:24,600
and the seas of the planet. The planktose team

714
00:37:24,700 --> 00:37:27,200
is going to feed plankton beds with a nutrient

715
00:37:27,300 --> 00:37:31,340
the microscopic creatures need, iron.

716
00:37:31,440 --> 00:37:34,700
The more plankton in the ocean, the less CO2

717
00:37:34,701 --> 00:37:37,900
in the atmosphere. For every ton of iron

718
00:37:37,901 --> 00:37:42,200
that goes into the ocean, about 100,000 tons of carbon dioxide

719
00:37:42,201 --> 00:37:46,440
is taken out of the atmosphere by the ocean plants.

720
00:37:46,441 --> 00:37:48,540
Using satellite imagery, the team

721
00:37:48,541 --> 00:37:50,780
has identified a pristine plankton bloom

722
00:37:50,781 --> 00:37:56,480
for their experiment 300 miles west of the Galapagos Islands.

723
00:37:56,481 --> 00:37:58,620
Here I have a sampling of some of the iron nutrient

724
00:37:58,621 --> 00:38:00,360
that we'll be mixing into the water.

725
00:38:00,361 --> 00:38:03,360
And this iron nutrient that we're going to be fertilizing

726
00:38:03,361 --> 00:38:05,860
fine to where when we drop it into the water,

727
00:38:05,960 --> 00:38:07,360
it's going to dissolve.

728
00:38:07,460 --> 00:38:10,160
And we want it to float on the surface for as long as possible.

729
00:38:10,260 --> 00:38:13,100
That way, it'll draw the plankton to the surface.

730
00:38:13,200 --> 00:38:15,440
Over a two-week period, the Weatherbird crew

731
00:38:15,540 --> 00:38:19,880
will use a plastic tube to dump between 50 and 100 tons of iron

732
00:38:19,980 --> 00:38:22,840
atop the Galapagos bloom.

733
00:38:22,940 --> 00:38:26,820
According to Planktose, that will remove 5 to 10 million tons

734
00:38:26,920 --> 00:38:29,880
of CO2 from the atmosphere.

735
00:38:29,980 --> 00:38:31,580
We use these mixers here.

736
00:38:31,581 --> 00:38:33,540
We suck up seawater into them.

737
00:38:33,640 --> 00:38:35,180
They can hold 300 gallons.

738
00:38:35,280 --> 00:38:36,420
We open this up.

739
00:38:36,520 --> 00:38:38,320
We put the iron in here.

740
00:38:38,420 --> 00:38:40,220
Motors turn on.

741
00:38:40,320 --> 00:38:42,420
Mix the iron up with the seawater.

742
00:38:42,520 --> 00:38:45,220
This in turn, these tubes come to the back,

743
00:38:45,320 --> 00:38:48,260
and we release that concentrated iron seawater mix

744
00:38:48,360 --> 00:38:52,140
while the boat's moving and spread it over the ocean.

745
00:38:52,240 --> 00:38:54,540
After spreading the iron, a research team

746
00:38:54,640 --> 00:38:56,680
will stay with the bloom for 20 weeks

747
00:38:56,780 --> 00:38:59,740
to monitor plankton growth and to record how much carbon

748
00:38:59,741 --> 00:39:04,240
much carbon dioxide it has absorbed.

749
00:39:04,241 --> 00:39:07,340
They'll test the samples to determine how deep the CO2-filled

750
00:39:07,341 --> 00:39:10,580
plankton has sunk. This enables the team to put a

751
00:39:10,581 --> 00:39:14,780
time frame on how long the CO2 will remain out of the atmosphere.

752
00:39:14,781 --> 00:39:18,420
Once we dry it, we can identify how much carbon is present in

753
00:39:18,421 --> 00:39:21,020
the dried biomass. And we'll do that for different

754
00:39:21,021 --> 00:39:24,540
depths so we can see how much is at the surface, how much is sunk

755
00:39:24,541 --> 00:39:28,160
to 100 meters, 200 meters. And as it goes further down, we'll

756
00:39:28,161 --> 00:39:31,560
has been sequestered for decades, hundreds of years,

757
00:39:31,660 --> 00:39:33,200
or millennia.

758
00:39:33,300 --> 00:39:34,960
The deeper the plankton goes,

759
00:39:35,060 --> 00:39:37,200
the more pressure there is to hold it down

760
00:39:37,300 --> 00:39:39,700
and keep its carbon from rising.

761
00:39:39,800 --> 00:39:42,900
If it sinks below 500 meters, it will stay there

762
00:39:43,000 --> 00:39:46,320
for upwards of 1,000 years.

763
00:39:46,420 --> 00:39:48,820
And that means more money for planktose

764
00:39:48,920 --> 00:39:51,820
that can be made by selling carbon credits.

765
00:39:51,920 --> 00:39:53,860
Yes, it's about money too.

766
00:39:53,960 --> 00:39:56,860
Carbon credits are a way for CO2-producing industries

767
00:39:56,861 --> 00:40:00,700
and individuals to offset their own emissions.

768
00:40:00,800 --> 00:40:03,260
By paying companies like Planktose to plant iron

769
00:40:03,360 --> 00:40:05,600
in the ocean, trees in the ground,

770
00:40:05,700 --> 00:40:10,100
or wind turbines in the sky, we might give our Earth

771
00:40:10,200 --> 00:40:13,100
a fighting chance.

772
00:40:13,200 --> 00:40:18,920
The worldwide standard is one credit equals one ton of CO2.

773
00:40:19,020 --> 00:40:21,520
Prices vary depending on method.

774
00:40:21,620 --> 00:40:24,760
By restoring the plankton blooms that are in great decline

775
00:40:24,761 --> 00:40:27,960
decline in the ocean, we can bring that life back.

776
00:40:28,060 --> 00:40:31,360
And in the bargain, we earn carbon credits, which

777
00:40:31,460 --> 00:40:33,060
pay for the whole operation.

778
00:40:33,160 --> 00:40:36,600
So it's a wonderful win-win situation for everybody.

779
00:40:36,700 --> 00:40:40,000
Many in the marine science community disagree.

780
00:40:40,100 --> 00:40:42,940
To some, the planktose project may have unforeseen

781
00:40:43,040 --> 00:40:45,020
consequences.

782
00:40:45,120 --> 00:40:48,960
When you suddenly cause a bloom of plankton or algae

783
00:40:49,060 --> 00:40:51,460
to occur where it otherwise wasn't going to occur,

784
00:40:51,560 --> 00:40:54,560
that growth will absorb oxygen, which might rob

785
00:40:54,561 --> 00:40:57,200
that particular section of the ocean of oxygen for fish

786
00:40:57,300 --> 00:40:59,000
or other things that need to live there.

787
00:40:59,100 --> 00:41:01,160
So as with any genie in the bottle,

788
00:41:01,260 --> 00:41:04,940
we have to release it carefully, if at all.

789
00:41:05,040 --> 00:41:06,640
And although there's no consensus

790
00:41:06,740 --> 00:41:09,040
on how much carbon the ocean can hold,

791
00:41:09,140 --> 00:41:12,280
for planktos, the experiment is worth the risks.

792
00:41:12,380 --> 00:41:15,820
Our intention is to discover whether it's okay to do this.

793
00:41:15,920 --> 00:41:17,220
And of course, if it doesn't work,

794
00:41:17,320 --> 00:41:19,180
we won't be able to earn any money for it.

795
00:41:19,280 --> 00:41:21,620
And since we're a for-profit company,

796
00:41:21,720 --> 00:41:23,420
our investors will stop funding us,

797
00:41:23,421 --> 00:41:26,160
and everything will come to a halt.

798
00:41:26,260 --> 00:41:27,860
Other green machines on our oceans

799
00:41:27,960 --> 00:41:31,920
are less controversial and a little more lofty,

800
00:41:32,020 --> 00:41:36,960
like this German product called SkySail.

801
00:41:37,060 --> 00:41:39,160
It's being tested as a way to reduce emissions

802
00:41:39,260 --> 00:41:42,380
from the thousands of cargo ships that chug across our seas

803
00:41:42,480 --> 00:41:44,580
every day.

804
00:41:44,680 --> 00:41:46,580
The SkySail is meant to take some of the burden

805
00:41:46,680 --> 00:41:49,380
off a ship's engine.

806
00:41:49,480 --> 00:41:52,520
Tethered to the deck with a web of carbon fiber ropes,

807
00:41:52,521 --> 00:41:56,420
It gives the ship an added boost to pull it through the water.

808
00:41:56,520 --> 00:41:58,620
You might not turn off the engines all the time,

809
00:41:58,720 --> 00:42:00,560
but you can certainly reduce the amount of fuel

810
00:42:00,660 --> 00:42:02,760
that you're burning and the number of hours

811
00:42:02,860 --> 00:42:05,360
that you are burning them.

812
00:42:05,460 --> 00:42:06,860
Of course, to reduce engine hours,

813
00:42:06,960 --> 00:42:08,560
the sky sail must catch the wind.

814
00:42:12,340 --> 00:42:14,480
A computer attached to the towing cable

815
00:42:14,580 --> 00:42:18,520
reads wind speed and direction, and then guides the sail up

816
00:42:18,620 --> 00:42:21,120
or down to the best air.

817
00:42:21,121 --> 00:42:25,860
The sail can fly as high as 950 feet, where strong sustained winds

818
00:42:25,960 --> 00:42:28,320
prevail.

819
00:42:28,420 --> 00:42:31,160
Onboard computers coordinate satellite weather data

820
00:42:31,260 --> 00:42:35,300
with shipping lanes for the fastest route.

821
00:42:35,400 --> 00:42:38,980
Tests aboard the 180-foot research vessel Beaufort

822
00:42:39,080 --> 00:42:41,940
proves the system flies.

823
00:42:42,040 --> 00:42:45,640
In 10-mile-per-hour winds, the 80 square meter sky sail

824
00:42:45,740 --> 00:42:49,820
produced a towing force of three tons.

825
00:42:49,821 --> 00:42:55,360
That equated to a savings of 320 gallons of ship diesel per day.

826
00:42:55,460 --> 00:42:58,620
If the skysail was placed on just half the 40,000 cargo

827
00:42:58,720 --> 00:43:02,560
ships and tankers on the sea, it would save over 2 billion

828
00:43:02,660 --> 00:43:05,760
gallons of diesel per year.

829
00:43:05,860 --> 00:43:08,080
With larger sails and higher winds,

830
00:43:08,180 --> 00:43:10,980
the savings would increase.

831
00:43:11,080 --> 00:43:13,580
When the winds die down or a storm approaches,

832
00:43:13,680 --> 00:43:16,380
the skysail is hauled into a safe berth below deck.

833
00:43:16,381 --> 00:43:21,120
As we sail into the 21st century,

834
00:43:21,220 --> 00:43:24,320
we look to green machines to help save our environment.

835
00:43:24,420 --> 00:43:28,320
I think technology will make the planet greener.

836
00:43:28,420 --> 00:43:29,920
Technology is already saving us.

837
00:43:30,020 --> 00:43:31,720
Obviously, we have a long way to go,

838
00:43:31,820 --> 00:43:34,440
but I'm not one of these doom and gloom people.

839
00:43:34,540 --> 00:43:36,940
Well, I'm very optimistic.

840
00:43:37,040 --> 00:43:38,940
With a healthy dose of optimism

841
00:43:39,040 --> 00:43:42,280
and some innovative thinking, we may have the fuel

842
00:43:42,380 --> 00:43:46,380
to ensure a clean and prosperous future.

